  note    = {The {IUPHAR} histamine receptor review that docs/open\_decisions.md 9.79 cites for the {H2} receptor's transducers, the successor to hill1997iupharhistamine. The full text is in PubMed Central ({PMC4485016}), read 27 October 2026 with experiments/read\_public\_page.py, and the two sentences 9.79 quotes are confirmed there, in its {H2} signal transduction section: "As for many adenylyl cyclase-coupled {GPCRs}, the {H2} receptor stimulates {cAMP} production via the coupling to {Gs} proteins, as indicated by, for example, agonist-induced photoaffinity labeling" and "The {H2} receptor does not only couple to {Gs} proteins but also to {Gq/11} proteins, resulting in inositol phosphate formation and increases in cytosolic {Ca2+} concentration in some, but not all, {H2} receptor expressing cells (for previous reviews, see Hill, 1990, and Hill et al., 1997)" ({PMC} full text, read 27 October 2026; {PMC}'s plain text renders subscripts and superscripts as plain characters, so a quote carried into a living document is to be rechecked against the typeset {PDF}). The {Gq/11} sentence itself cites only the two Hill reviews, and 9.79 records that hill1997iupharhistamine, read in full on 26 October 2026, does not support it; Hill 1990 is not held. The sentence that follows it in the same paragraph, read on Fable on 2 November 2026, does cite primary work, and all of it is heterologous: "Moreover, photoaffinity labeling studies with [ $\gamma$ - 32 P]GTP azidoanilide revealed that H 2 receptor can activate both mammalian and insect cell G q proteins in addition to G s proteins ( Kuhn et al., 1996 ; Leopoldt et al., 1997 ), whereas heterologous expression of H 2 receptors in rat cardiomyocytes has also been reported to result in the interaction of recombinantly expressed H 2 receptors with G q/11 , next to G s proteins ( Wellner-Kienitz et al., 2003 )". So the review's whole case for {Gq/11} is those three papers, which 9.79 already weighs, and the review labels the third heterologous and recombinant in its own words. Two further things the full section says, recorded in docs/label\_source\_review.md section 41: the review's evidence for {Gs} at the {G}-protein level is also heterologous (photoaffinity labelling "in mammalian and insect cell expression systems", and receptor-{G}$\alpha$ fusion proteins, Kelley et al. 2001 and Wenzel-Seifert et al. 2001), what is native for {Gs} being the cyclase and {cAMP} response, "cAMP formation in membrane preparations from various tissues as well as cAMP accumulation in various native cells"; and the paragraph stating the {Gq/11} coupling carries the curators' own caution that in that same system {Gs} raises calcium by itself, "Interestingly, in this cell system also the G s -cAMP-PKO pathway promotes increases in cytosolic Ca 2+ concentration via the phosphorylation of L-type Ca 2+ channels ( Wellner-Kienitz et al., 2003 )" - where "PKO" is what the {PMC} text reads, verified in the raw {HTML} and almost certainly a deposit error for {PKA}, so that quotation above all is to be rechecked against the typeset {PDF}. Nothing else from the full text is asserted here. Review. In {PMC} (Europe {PMC} inPMC Y, {PMC4485016}) but NOT in Europe {PMC}'s open-access subset (isOpenAccess N); the {PMC} record states "Copyright \copyright{} 2015 by The American Society for Pharmacology and Experimental Therapeutics" and names no licence, so the licence is unverified}
