  note    = {Found by a PubMed search for the {HRH2} transducer question of docs/open\_decisions.md 9.79; a native-cell (purified rat {Leydig} cell) and tumour-line ({MA-10} murine {Leydig} cell) study. "Our results regarding signal transduction pathways associated with these receptors indicate the coupling of {HRH2} to the adenylate cyclase system through direct interaction with a {Gs} protein. Moreover, we show {HRH1} activation mediates increases in inositol phosphate production, possibly due to coupling of this receptor to {Gq} protein and phospholipase {C} activation" (abstract, PubMed 15917347, read 27 October 2026). THE FULL TEXT IS NOW HELD, read on Fable 5.1 on 31 October 2026. The owner did not supply it; it was reached instead at the authors' institutional repository, {CONICET} Digital, whose item (handle 11336/31465, {DSpace} item 284f9ccf-ea62-4ede-a5bb-d91606b1613c) carries the publisher's typeset version (dc.type info:eu-repo/semantics/publishedVersion, paginated 899-907 as {PubMed} has it), fetched with experiments/read\_public\_page.py on 31 October 2026 (216,355 bytes, {SHA-256} 9674b77ece8138cfa0ea4cdf4faaf944ba68b46602ddcc6e472df7d3202ab241). {BioOne}, which carries the journal's 2005 volume, serves the abstract and puts the body behind a subscription; there is no {PMC} copy. THE FULL TEXT CORRECTS HOW THE ABSTRACT SENTENCE ABOVE IS TO BE READ: the paper contains no measurement at the {G}-protein level at all. A search of the whole text for "cholera", "pertussis", "{ADP}-ribos", "photoaffin", "immunoprecip", "{GTP}" and "[35{S}]" returns nothing, and the only antibodies in it are those for {cAMP}, progesterone and testosterone (p. 900), so the abstract's "direct interaction with a {Gs} protein" rests on agonist-induced {cAMP} accumulation and nothing more direct, which is how the Discussion itself puts it: "Moreover, {HRH2} agonist-induced augmentation of {cAMP} production indicates {HRH2} couples to the adenylate cyclase ({AC}) system in Leydig cells" (p. 905). WHAT IT ADDS FOR 9.79 is a negative for an {H2} phospholipase {C} arm in cells expressing the receptor endogenously: "{HT} at 10-7 {M} produced a 2.3-fold increase in the levels of {IP}; 10-10 {M} {HT} also elicited a significant augmentation of {IP}, although less pronounced. {DIM}, however, had no significant effect" (p. 903), where {HT} is the {HRH1} agonist {HTMT}-dimaleate, {DIM} the {HRH2} agonist dimaprit at 10-6 {M}, and 10 m{M} {NaF} the positive control at more than 4-fold; the same {DIM} concentration raised basal {cAMP} and tripled the {hCG}-stimulated {cAMP} response in the same cells (p. 903). Three limits on that negative: it was run in the model with the lower {H2} density ([3{H}]-tiotidine {Bmax} 0.36 fmol/mg protein in {MA-10} cells, p. 902, against 100.4 fmol/mg in the purified rat Leydig cells, p. 904, which were never tested for inositol phosphates); the authors themselves read a potency difference between the two models as receptor number, "far lower concentrations of {DIM} were needed to elicit stimulation of steroidogenesis in these cells (Fig. 10B), possibly due to the much higher number of {HRH2} binding sites compared to {MA-10} cells" (p. 904); and both densities are fmol/mg protein rather than sites per cell, so neither can be set against zhu1994dualsignaling's 100,000 to 300,000 sites/cell without an assumption. One dimaprit concentration, 30 min, no antagonist control on the inositol phosphate assay (famotidine and pyrilamine were used only against progesterone output, p. 903), and the readout is total inositol phosphates, not {IP3}. The receptors are mouse ({MA-10} Leydig tumour line) and rat (purified Leydig cells), not human, as with kuehn1996hrh2gq and leopoldt1997sf9gproteins. The paper is not an independent line into the {Gq/11} question: its introduction takes the assignment as settled, "The presently known {HA} receptors ({HRH1}, {HRH2}, and {HRH3}) are all {G} protein-coupled molecules, which transduce extracellular signals via {Gq}, {Gs}, and {Gi/o}, respectively [7]" (p. 899, citing Hough, Mol Pharmacol 2001;59:415-419), and its agonist and antagonist potencies are taken from its reference 19, which is hill1997iupharhistamine. Not open access at the publisher (Europe {PMC} isOpenAccess N, inPMC N, no {PMC} identifier); year 2005 is the journal-issue year, and the Europe {PMC} record gives the electronic publication as 25 May 2005 and the issue as November 2005, as does the repository's dc.date.issued of 1 November 2005. LICENCE, both sides of it, read 31 October 2026: the typeset article carries a copyright line only, "\copyright{} 2005 by the Society for the Study of Reproduction, Inc." with "{ISSN}: 0006-3363. http://www.biolreprod.org" (p. 899), and a search of the whole text for "creative", "{CC} {BY}", "open access" and "reuse" returns nothing, so THE PUBLISHER'S LICENCE IS UNVERIFIED (confirmed by a second route: {PubMed}'s copyright record for this {PMID} holds no statement, no licence and is\_open\_access false); the deposited copy is open access under the repository's own licence, its {DSpace} item record giving dc.rights "info:eu-repo/semantics/openAccess" and dc.rights.uri "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/2.5/ar/", that is {CC} {BY-NC-SA} 2.5 Argentina. Only quoted sentences are carried into the repository; the {PDF} is not committed}
