  note    = {The paper docs/open\_decisions.md 9.79 called "the highest-value outstanding item for this row and for 9.20": it is reference 19 of K{\"u}hn et al. 1996, cited there for the claim that cholera toxin does not selectively inhibit {Gs}-coupled stimulation of phospholipase {C}, and the paper 9.20's addition of 26 October 2026 used for a sites-per-cell threshold. FULL TEXT HELD: the publisher's {PDF}, supplied by the owner on 31 October 2026 after every open route was refused that day (no {PMC} or Europe {PMC} record, molpharm.aspetjournals.org disallowed by its robots file, www.sciencedirect.com {HTTP} 403, {OpenAlex} closed with no repository copy; docs/label\_source\_review.md section 39), pinned at data/raw/supplied\_papers/pmid7935326\_zhu1994.pdf ({SHA}-256 9b56f48e..., 10 pages, printed 460-469, not committed) and read in full on Fable the same day. Not about histamine: the word does not occur in it. Four {Gs}-coupled receptors, murine {LHR}, human {V2R}, human beta1 and beta2 adrenergic, stably transfected into {Ltk}- fibroblasts at 500 to 500,000 sites/cell (Table 1, p. 462, Scatchard analysis of intact cells); readouts fura-2 calcium, [3{H}]inositol phosphates and adenylyl cyclase. The {PLC} arm appears at a receptor-specific density: "The coupling to {PLC} was clearly evident in {LHR} cells expressing 4000 sites/cell, whereas such coupling was not detected for {V2R} cells expressing 20,000 sites/cell or beta2{AR} cells expressing 120,000 sites/cell" (p. 468), the {V2R} "at 100,000 sites/cell or more and the beta{ARs} at 300,000 sites/cell" (p. 460); so the "100,000 to 300,000 sites/cell" earlier quoted from the abstract here and in 9.20 is the {V2R} and beta{AR} figure only. Only the beta{AR} lines are also given per milligram, "0.4 to 3.2 pmol/mg of membrane protein" (p. 465). Cholera toxin, one receptor and one line, {LHR20/20} at 40,000 sites/cell, 16 h pretreatment: "treatment with either 1 or 10 $\mu$g/ml toxin did not affect the basal level of {IP} accumulation but resulted in approximately 50% inhibition of both h{CG}- and {ATP}-stimulated activities (Fig. 6b). Because {ATP} does not activate {Gs} in these cells, the inhibition of agonist-stimulated {PLC} activities by {CTX} seems to be a rather general effect not related specifically to the {Gs}-coupled receptors" (p. 465); "was not preferentially inhibited by this toxin, because {ATP}-stimulated accumulation of {IPs} was also inhibited to the same extent whenever an inhibitory effect was seen" (p. 468). So K{\"u}hn's reading of reference 19 is supported by the paper itself, and the question left open on 31 October 2026, whether K{\"u}hn had read Liao et al. 1990's calcium result ({PMID} 2162842; cited by Zhu only for the fura-2 method, its reference 20) as an inositol one, is closed; "{Gq}-coupled" for the {P2} receptor is K{\"u}hn's gloss, not Zhu's wording. No transducer is identified for the second arm: pertussis toxin insensitive by citation (pp. 467-468), {Gq}-family alpha subunits and free beta-gamma both left open (p. 467), and "Caution should be used when interpreting dual coupling results in physiological terms" (p. 467). The PubMed record carries no {DOI}; the one above is Elsevier's, recorded by Europe {PMC} and carried in the {PDF}'s own metadata; PubMed writes the pages 460-9 and Europe {PMC} 460-469, the same range. Received 13 January 1994, accepted 23 June 1994 (p. 460). Journal article, not a preprint. Not open access. Licence read on the paper: "Copyright \copyright{} by The American Society for Pharmacology and Experimental Therapeutics. All rights of reproduction in any form reserved" (p. 460) -- all rights reserved, so quotation only and no table or figure is copied}
